```

The attempt-2 mismatch is between the t4 term (D^9) and the t5 term (D^11) at level 5. The t5 term carries extra scale history because z, from a much lower level, is subtracted before the c5 multiply. I now apply the coefficient to the double product first and subtract z·c5 separately. This is still a guess about how CHEHAB tracks scales.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext s = square(z);
  Ciphertext t2 = s + s - one;
  Ciphertext w3 = z * t2;
  Ciphertext t3 = w3 + w3 - z;
  Ciphertext s4 = square(t2);
  Ciphertext t4a = s4 + s4 - one;
  Ciphertext w4 = z * t3;
  Ciphertext t4b = w4 + w4 - t2;
  Ciphertext w5 = t2 * t3;
  Ciphertext s6 = square(t3);
  Ciphertext t6 = s6 + s6 - one;
  Ciphertext w7 = t3 * t4a;
  Plaintext c0("c0"), c1("c1"), c2("c2"), c3("c3"), c4("c4"), c5("c5"), c6("c6"), c7("c7");
  Ciphertext zc1 = z * c1;
  Ciphertext zc57 = z * c5;
  Ciphertext zc7 = z * c7;
  Ciphertext u5 = w5 * c5;
  Ciphertext u7 = w7 * c7;
  Ciphertext B = u5 + u5 + u7 + u7 - zc57 - zc7;
  Ciphertext result = zc1;
  result = result + t2 * c2;
  result = result + t3 * c3;
  result = result + t4b * c4;
  result = result + t6 * c6;
  result = result + B;
  result = result + c0;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

The scale exponents tell the story. A term's exponent is the sum of its operands' exponents, plus one for the coefficient multiply. Terms that land at the same level therefore end up with different exponents.

The terms below are written as (level, exponent):

| Term | (level, exponent) |
|---|---|
| z·c1 | (2, 3) |
| t2·c2 | (3, 5) |
| t3·c3 | (4, 7) |
| t4·c4 | (4, 9) |
| t5·c5 | (5, 11) |
| t6·c6 | (5, 13) |
| t7·c7 | (5, 15) |

Multiplying a term by the plaintext `one` raises its level by one, which puts it at a level of its own. CHEHAB then aligns different levels for free. I push the later terms by 1, 1, 2 and 3 levels so every term meets the others at a distinct level. This costs extra plaintext multiplies at deep levels, which are cheap. It uses only 6 ciphertext multiplications, one fewer than attempt 2.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Plaintext c0("c0"), c1("c1"), c2("c2"), c3("c3"), c4("c4"), c5("c5"), c6("c6"), c7("c7");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext s = square(z);
  Ciphertext t2 = s + s - one;
  Ciphertext w3 = z * t2;
  Ciphertext t3 = w3 + w3 - z;
  Ciphertext s4 = square(t2);
  Ciphertext t4 = s4 + s4 - one;
  Ciphertext w5 = t2 * t3;
  Ciphertext t5 = w5 + w5 - z;
  Ciphertext s6 = square(t3);
  Ciphertext t6 = s6 + s6 - one;
  Ciphertext w7 = t3 * t4;
  Ciphertext t7 = w7 + w7 - z;

  Ciphertext r1 = z * c1;
  Ciphertext r2 = t2 * c2;
  Ciphertext r3 = t3 * c3;
  Ciphertext r4 = (t4 * c4) * one;
  Ciphertext r5 = (t5 * c5) * one;
  Ciphertext r6 = ((t6 * c6) * one) * one;
  Ciphertext r7 = (((t7 * c7) * one) * one) * one;

  Ciphertext result = r1 + r2;
  result = result + r3;
  result = result + r4;
  result = result + r5;
  result = result + r6;
  result = result + r7;
  result = result + c0;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

This is a different circuit from the baseline: split the polynomial into even and odd parts in v = T2(z) = 2z² − 1.
- Even part: E = c0 + c2·v + c4·(2v² − 1) + c6·(4v³ − 3v).
- Odd part: z·B, where B = (c1−c3−c5+c7) + v·(2c3−2c5−4c7) + v²·(4c5−4c7) + 8c7·v³.
- All coefficient combinations are built with ciphertext adds and subtracts, since plaintext-plaintext arithmetic isn't allowed.
- This needs only 4 ciphertext multiplications (z², v², v³, z·B) against 6 in the baseline.
- The depth drops to 6 levels.
- It uses more plaintext multiplies (11), but the terms land at distinct levels, which avoids the scale mismatches.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Plaintext c0("c0"), c1("c1"), c2("c2"), c3("c3"), c4("c4"), c5("c5"), c6("c6"), c7("c7");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext u = square(z);
  Ciphertext v = u + u - one;
  Ciphertext v2 = square(v);
  Ciphertext v3 = v * v2;

  // v-level terms
  Ciphertext v_c2 = v * c2;
  Ciphertext v_c3 = v * c3;
  Ciphertext v_c5 = v * c5;
  Ciphertext v_c6 = v * c6;
  Ciphertext v_c7 = v * c7;
  // v^2 terms
  Ciphertext w_c4 = v2 * c4;
  Ciphertext w_c5 = v2 * c5;
  Ciphertext w_c7 = v2 * c7;
  // v^3 terms
  Ciphertext x_c6 = v3 * c6;
  Ciphertext x_c7 = v3 * c7;

  // Even part
  Ciphertext e1 = v_c2 - v_c6 - v_c6 - v_c6;
  Ciphertext e2 = w_c4 + w_c4;
  Ciphertext e3 = x_c6 + x_c6;
  e3 = e3 + e3;
  Ciphertext E = e1 + e2;
  E = E + e3;
  E = E + c0;
  E = E - c4;

  // Odd bracket
  Ciphertext b1 = v_c3 + v_c3 - v_c5 - v_c5 - v_c7 - v_c7 - v_c7 - v_c7;
  Ciphertext b2 = w_c5 - w_c7;
  b2 = b2 + b2;
  b2 = b2 + b2;
  Ciphertext b3 = x_c7 + x_c7;
  b3 = b3 + b3;
  b3 = b3 + b3;
  b3 = b3 + b3 - b3 + b3 - b3 + b3 - b3 + b3;
  Ciphertext B = b1 + b2;
  B = B + b3;
  B = B + c1;
  B = B - c3;
  B = B - c5;
  B = B + c7;

  Ciphertext zB = z * B;
  Ciphertext result = zB + E;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

Wait — the b3 line above is wrong. b3 should be 8·x_c7 = three doublings, and that line adds extra terms. Corrected kernel:

